.TH "GAMMU-SMSD-RUN" "7" "Sep 16, 2026" "1.45.0" "Gammu"
.SH NAME
gammu-smsd-run \- documentation for RunOnReceive directive
.SH DESCRIPTION
.sp
Gammu SMSD can be configured by \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> directive (see
SMSD Configuration File \%<#\:gammu-smsdrc> for details) to run defined program after receiving
every message. It can receive single message or more messages, which are parts
of one multipart message.
.sp
The configured command can include parameters. SMSD appends identifiers of
received messages as additional arguments. The identifiers depend on the
service backend: typically database row IDs or file names for the files backend.
File names do not include the inbox directory. There can be multiple arguments
for a multipart message, so scripts must handle all supplied identifiers.
.sp
On POSIX systems, the configured command is executed through a shell, so special
characters in that command need to be escaped. Message identifiers are passed
as separate literal arguments. SMSD waits for the script to terminate, so slow
scripts can delay receiving new messages. Do not rely on a fixed timeout to
interrupt a blocked script.
.sp
On Windows, SMSD starts the configured executable directly. Batch files require
an explicit command interpreter, as shown in Running a batch file on Windows\&. SMSD does
not wait for the process to finish or collect its exit status. Hooks can overlap,
and successful process creation does not mean that message forwarding succeeded.
.sp
\fBNote:\fP
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
On POSIX systems, standard input is closed and standard output and standard
error are captured in the SMSD log. On Windows, the hook must arrange its own
output and error logging. Scripts should not require interactive input.
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.sp
Added in version 1.28.0.

.sp
Program is executed with environment which contains lot of information about
the message. You can use it together with NULL service (see
Null Backend \%<#\:gammu-smsd-null>) to implement completely own processing of messages.
.SS Global variables
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_MESSAGES
Number of physical messages received.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_PARTS
Number of decoded message parts.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B PHONE_ID
Added in version 1.38.2.

.sp
Value of \fBPhoneID\fP \%<#\:option-PhoneID>\&. Useful when running multiple instances
(see Multiple modems \%<#\:smsd-multi>). This variable is available to all \fBRunOn\fP
directives, including calls which do not provide message details.
.UNINDENT
.SS Per message variables
.sp
The variables further described as \fBSMS_1_...\fP are generated for each physical
message, where 1 is replaced by current number of message.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_CLASS
Class of message.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_NUMBER
Sender number.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_TEXT
Message text. Text is not available for 8\-bit binary messages.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_REFERENCE
Added in version 1.38.5.

.sp
Message Reference. If delivery status received, this variable contains TPMR of original message
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_SENT_ID
For a delivery report matched by a database backend, the ID of the
corresponding row in the \fBsentitems\fP table. The value is empty for other
messages, when no matching sent message was found, and for non\-database
backends.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_TIMESTAMP
Message date and time as seconds since the Unix epoch. For a received
message this is the service center timestamp. For a delivery report this is
the time when the service center received the original message. The value
is empty when the message does not contain a valid date and time.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_DATETIME
The same value as \fBSMS_1_TIMESTAMP\fP, formatted as ISO 8601 with a
timezone offset, for example \fB2024\-01\-02T03:04:05+01:30\fP\&.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_SMSC_TIMESTAMP
For a delivery report, the discharge time as seconds since the Unix epoch.
The value is empty for other messages or when the report does not contain a
valid discharge time.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B SMS_1_SMSC_DATETIME
The same value as \fBSMS_1_SMSC_TIMESTAMP\fP, formatted as ISO 8601
with a timezone offset.
.UNINDENT
.SS Per part variables
.sp
The variables further described as \fBDECODED_1_...\fP are generated for each message
part, where 1 is replaced by current number of part. Set are only those
variables whose content is present in the message.
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_1_TEXT
Decoded long message text.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_1_MMS_SENDER
Sender of MMS indication message.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_1_MMS_TITLE
title of MMS indication message.
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_1_MMS_ADDRESS
Address (URL) of MMS from MMS indication message.
.sp
\fBSee also:\fP
.INDENT 7.0
.INDENT 3.5
Can I use Gammu to receive MMS? \%<#\:faq-mms-download>
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.INDENT 0.0
.TP
.B DECODED_1_MMS_SIZE
Size of MMS as specified in MMS indication message.
.UNINDENT
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Activating RunOnReceive
.sp
To activate this feature you need to set \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> in
the SMSD Configuration File \%<#\:gammu-smsdrc>\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[smsd]
RunOnReceive = /path/to/script.sh
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Running a batch file on Windows
.sp
Use \fBcmd.exe\fP to launch a batch file. For example, with the script and inbox
in \fBC:\egammu\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
[smsd]
RunOnReceive = C:\eWindows\eSystem32\ecmd.exe /d /c C:\egammu\eforward_sms.bat
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Adjust these absolute paths to match your installation and restart SMSD after
changing the configuration. The example uses paths without spaces.
.sp
Do not add \fB\(dq%FILE%\(dq\fP: this is not a Gammu placeholder. SMSD appends the
received file names automatically. For example, the batch file receives
\fBIN20260228_053040_00_+61402111111_00.txt\fP as its first argument. In the batch
file, \fB%1\fP accesses that argument and \fB%~1\fP removes surrounding quotes. With
the inbox above, the full path is \fBC:\egammu\e%~1\fP\&. Process subsequent arguments
as well, for example using \fBshift\fP in a loop.
.sp
Use absolute paths for the inbox, address book, log files, and helper programs.
The hook inherits SMSD\(aqs working directory, which need not be the script or inbox
directory. When moving SMSD to a Windows service, ensure the service account can
access these files and any credentials needed by the forwarding program.
.sp
To test invocation separately from email delivery, save the following as
\fBC:\egammu\ereceive\-test.bat\fP and temporarily use that path in \fBRunOnReceive\fP:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
@echo off
setlocal DisableDelayedExpansion
>>C:\egammu\ereceive\-hook.log echo Hook started
>>C:\egammu\ereceive\-hook.log set SMS_MESSAGES
>>C:\egammu\ereceive\-hook.log set SMS_1_NUMBER
.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.sp
Restart SMSD and send a fresh SMS. A new entry in \fBreceive\-hook.log\fP confirms
that the batch file started. If no entry appears, check the SMSD log for
\fBStarting run on receive\fP and \fBCreateProcess failed\fP, and check that the
account running SMSD can write the diagnostic log. Restore the forwarding script
path after testing. Its own log should record processing and email delivery
errors because Windows SMSD does not collect the script\(aqs output or exit status.
.SS Forwarding messages with an address book
.sp
A forwarding program can match \fBSMS_1_NUMBER\fP against the \fBNumber\fP
column of a CSV address book and use the corresponding \fBName\fP in the email
subject. Store and compare phone numbers in a consistent format, for example
international numbers beginning with \fB+\fP, and use the sender\(aqs number when no
name matches.
.sp
For multipart text, use the available \fBDECODED_n_TEXT\fP variables described
above, or process all file arguments. Do not assume \fBSMS_1_TEXT\fP contains
the entire message. Read message text as data inside the forwarding program
rather than expanding it into batch commands, where SMS characters could be
interpreted as command syntax.
.sp
Configure SMTP authentication in the forwarding program. That program is also
responsible for logging delivery failures and retaining pending messages for
retry; starting a hook does not guarantee email delivery.
.SS Processing messages from the files backend
.sp
Following script (if used as \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> handler) passes
message data to other program. This works only with the Files backend \%<#\:gammu-smsd-files>\&.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh
INBOX=/path/to/smsd/inbox
PROGRAM=/bin/cat
for ID in \(dq$@\(dq ; do
    $PROGRAM < $INBOX/$ID
done

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Invoking commands based on message text
.sp
Following script (if used as \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> handler) executes
given programs based on message text.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh

# Check for sender number
if [ \(dq$SMS_1_NUMBER\(dq != \(dq+420123456789\(dq ] ; then
    exit
fi

# Handle commands
case \(dq$SMS_1_TEXT\(dq in
    \(dqDMS A\(dq)
        /usr/bin/dms\-a
        ;;
    \(dqDMS B\(dq)
        /usr/bin/dms\-b
        ;;
esac

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Passing message text to program
.sp
Following script (if used as \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> handler) passes
message text and sender to external program.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh
PROGRAM=/bin/echo
for i in \(gaseq $SMS_MESSAGES\(ga ; do
    eval \(dq$PROGRAM \e\(dq\e${SMS_${i}_NUMBER}\e\(dq \e\(dq\e${SMS_${i}_TEXT}\e\(dq\(dq
done

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Passing MMS indication parameters to external program
.sp
Following script (if used as \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> handler) will write
information about each received MMS indication to the log file. Just replace
echo command with your own program to do custom processing.
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/bin/sh
if [ $DECODED_PARTS \-eq 0 ] ; then
    # No decoded parts, nothing to process
    exit
fi
if [ \(dq$DECODED_1_MMS_ADDRESS\(dq ] ; then
    echo \(dq$DECODED_1_MMS_ADDRESS\(dq \(dq$DECODED_1_MMS_SENDER\(dq \(dq$DECODED_1_MMS_TITLE\(dq >> /tmp/smsd\-mms.log
fi

.EE
.UNINDENT
.UNINDENT
.SS Processing message text in Python
.sp
Following script (if used as \fBRunOnReceive\fP \%<#\:option-RunOnReceive> handler) written
in Python will concatenate all text from received message:
.INDENT 0.0
.INDENT 3.5
.sp
.EX
#!/usr/bin/env python

import os

numparts = int(os.environ[\(dqDECODED_PARTS\(dq])

text = \(dq\(dq
# Are there any decoded parts?
if numparts == 0:
    text = os.environ[\(dqSMS_1_TEXT\(dq]
# Get all text parts
else:
    for i in range(1, numparts + 1):
        varname = \(dqDECODED_%d_TEXT\(dq % i
        if varname in os.environ:
            text = text + os.environ[varname]

# Do something with the text
print(\(dqNumber {} have sent text: {}\(dq.format(os.environ[\(dqSMS_1_NUMBER\(dq], text))

.EE
